Video Consultation Service Implementation Plan
Responsibilities:

    Video Calls Between Clients and Veterinarians:
        Handle video consultations using WebRTC for real-time communication between clients and veterinarians.
        Provide necessary endpoints to initiate, join, and terminate video calls.

    Appointment Integration:
        Sync with the Appointment Service to allow scheduling and automatic initiation of video consultations based on appointments.

    Consultation Recording:
        Provide an option to record video consultations for future reference or for the pet owner's review.
        Allow veterinarians to access and store these recordings for medical records.

    Security and Authentication:
        Ensure that only authorized users (veterinarians and pet owners) can join the consultations.
        Implement secure access control for video streams and recordings.

    Notification and Alerts:
        Notify users via the Notification Service (if integrated) when a consultation is about to start, or when a recording is available for review.

Communication:

    Appointment Service:
        Why: The Video Consultation Service needs to synchronize with the Appointment Service to automatically set up video calls based on scheduled virtual appointments.
        What: Fetch appointment details and initiate video consultations at the time of the scheduled appointment.

    Pet Management Service:
        Why: The Pet Management Service stores medical records and pet profiles, which may need to be referenced during the video consultation.
        What: Provide pet medical records and history for consultation reference.

    Task & Resource Management Service:
        Why: The video consultation may generate tasks for veterinarians or require specific resources (like a follow-up task).
        What: Send tasks or follow-up procedures after video consultations.

    Notification Service:
        Why: Notify pet owners and veterinarians when video calls are about to start or when a consultation recording is available.
        What: Send notifications about upcoming consultations or availability of recordings.

Database:

    VideoConsultation:
        Fields: consultationId, appointmentId, vetId, petId, ownerId, startTime, endTime, recordingUrl, etc.
        Purpose: To store details of video consultations and associated metadata.

    Recording Management:
        Fields: recordingId, consultationId, url, storageLocation, etc.
        Purpose: Manage recordings of video consultations.

Step-by-Step Implementation Plan:

    Maven Setup:
        Set up a Maven project with necessary dependencies, such as Spring Boot, WebRTC libraries, and security/authentication tools.

    Database Schema:
        Create tables to store consultation records, manage consultation links, and track recordings.

    Video API:
        Endpoints to initiate, join, and terminate video consultations.
        Implement security and authentication for access to the video streams.

    Appointment Synchronization:
        Fetch virtual appointments from the Appointment Service and automatically schedule video calls.

    Consultation Recording:
        Add functionality to record video calls and store recordings securely.

    Notifications and Alerts:
        Integrate with the Notification Service to alert users about upcoming consultations and recording availability.

    Testing:
        Ensure integration with other microservices.
        Functional tests for video calls, recording, and synchronization.

Tech Stack for the Video Consultation Service:
1. Backend Framework: Spring Boot (Java)

    Why:
        Spring Boot provides rapid development with features like dependency injection, web services, REST APIs, and security, which will be crucial for managing video sessions, handling WebRTC integration, and security.
        It's compatible with other services in the system, ensuring uniformity and ease of integration with microservices.
    Features: Use Spring Security for authentication and authorization, and WebSocket support for real-time video communication.

2. Video Streaming and Real-Time Communication: WebRTC

    Why:
        WebRTC is a popular standard for real-time communication, allowing direct peer-to-peer video calls.
        It supports low-latency video/audio transmission and works well for telemedicine scenarios like this one.
    Usage:
        WebRTC will handle video calls, peer-to-peer communication between clients (veterinarians and pet owners).
        Use a TURN/STUN server (such as Coturn hosted on AWS) for connection establishment and NAT traversal.

3. Database: MongoDB (NoSQL)

    Why:
        MongoDB’s document-oriented nature makes it ideal for storing dynamic and structured data like video consultation metadata (e.g., recording URLs, timestamps).
        It allows easy integration for storing JSON-like data and efficiently handles potentially large consultation records with fast read/write performance.
    Features:
        Use it to store metadata for consultations (start/end times, participants) and recordings.

4. Storage for Video Recordings: AWS S3 (Amazon Simple Storage Service)

    Why:
        AWS S3 is a highly scalable and secure object storage service, ideal for storing large media files like video recordings.
        It provides global distribution, redundancy, and secure access with S3 pre-signed URLs for accessing video files.
    Features:
        Store video recordings from the consultations.
        Pre-signed URLs for temporary access to recordings by pet owners and veterinarians.

5. Authentication and Authorization: AWS Cognito or Spring Security with OAuth 2.0

    Why:
        AWS Cognito can handle user authentication and authorization for pet owners and veterinarians, integrating well with AWS services.
        If you prefer keeping everything inside Spring, you can use Spring Security with OAuth 2.0 to manage session-based authentication and user roles.
    Features:
        Role-based access control (veterinarian vs. pet owner) for joining video calls and accessing recordings.

6. Task Management/Message Queue: AWS SQS or RabbitMQ

    Why:
        For handling asynchronous tasks like notifications, event-driven architecture (initiating calls, updating appointment status), and task retries, AWS SQS or RabbitMQ (already in use in the ecosystem) would be suitable.
    Features:
        Use SQS or RabbitMQ to trigger notifications (before or after the video consultation) and ensure reliable message delivery for appointment synchronization.

7. Notifications: AWS SNS (Simple Notification Service) or RabbitMQ

    Why:
        AWS SNS can handle notifications across multiple services, allowing push notifications for upcoming consultations, recording availability, and event-driven alerts.
    Features:
        Use it to send SMS, email, or mobile push notifications for reminders and updates related to video consultations.

8. Video Processing: AWS MediaConvert

    Why:
        For video recording transcoding or post-processing (e.g., converting video formats, reducing video size for storage), AWS MediaConvert provides an on-demand, scalable solution.
    Features:
        This service can be invoked after a consultation to process and optimize recordings before storing them in S3.

9. Real-Time Notifications/Signaling: WebSocket or Socket.IO (via Spring Boot WebSockets)

    Why:
        WebRTC requires signaling (exchange of connection information) between participants to establish peer-to-peer connections.
        Spring Boot WebSocket or Socket.IO can handle this real-time exchange of data, facilitating seamless video consultations.
    Features:
        Use WebSockets for signaling (e.g., joining or leaving a session, starting a video call).

10. Infrastructure: Kubernetes (K8s) with AWS EKS

    Why:
        Kubernetes provides orchestration for containerized services, making it easy to scale the Video Consultation Service horizontally as demand grows.
        Use AWS EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) for simplified deployment, scaling, and management of microservices.
    Features:
        Automate the deployment, scaling, and management of containerized microservices for high availability and fault tolerance.

11. Logging and Monitoring: AWS CloudWatch or ELK Stack

    Why:
        AWS CloudWatch offers deep integration with AWS services and can track logs, monitor services, and set up alarms based on key metrics (e.g., call duration, recording issues).
        Alternatively, ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, and Kibana) can provide powerful real-time monitoring and log aggregation across your services.
    Features:
        Track performance metrics, service errors, and video quality monitoring.

Tech Stack Summary:

    Backend: Spring Boot (Java), WebRTC, Spring WebSockets
    Database: MongoDB (NoSQL)
    Storage: AWS S3 (for video recordings)
    Authentication: Spring Security with OAuth 2.0
    Queueing/Asynchronous Tasks: RabbitMQ
    Notifications: RabbitMQ
    Video Processing: AWS MediaConvert (for transcoding and processing)
    Container Orchestration: Kubernetes (AWS EKS)
    Logging & Monitoring: AWS CloudWatch or ELK Stack